{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:growth:reconciler","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:54d1f8c221667bb445c84e90af39ed7a72f75a50ed886db9b2002eed0af60379","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.9},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.9},{"probability":0.9,"value":2.9}]},"questionHash":"sha256:e1b53adbdbe1bb1ccef339035b6376b49668b9341050c684f480d5b347e316f6","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq3","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"PPP WEIGHTS FIRST, SINCE THEY DOMINATE. Panelist spread on China's share of the pair: 0.38 (Fermi) / 0.33 (Historian) / 0.295 (Scout) / 0.335 (Bayesian) / 0.37 (red-team). The Bayesian seat's rebuild is the best-documented and I ADOPT IT: world PPP GDP ~$195T, China ~19.5% = ~$38T, OECD-38 sums to ~41% of world = ~$80T, so China = 38/118 = 0.322 at the 2026 start. Fermi's $73T OECD-38 is too low; the Scout's 46-47%-of-world is too high. Because my China growth leg is faster than the Bayesian's, the drift is faster: I use w_China = 0.34, w_OECD = 0.66 at the 2034 midpoint.\n\nTHE TWO RED-TEAM HITS I MOVED FOR, BOTH REAL.\n(1) DOUBLE-COUNTED CHINA AGING DRAG. 2A's -2.7 pp/yr is an IMF WEO Apr-2025 Ch.2 figure averaged 2025-2050 against a NO-AGING counterfactual, and China's observed ~4.5-5% print is already produced by an economy whose working-age population peaked in 2013 and whose total population has fallen since 2022. All three Wave-1 seats subtracted it from a near-current run-rate; the Fermi seat made the error auditable by publishing its implication — a no-aging counterfactual of ~5.4%/yr, i.e. the claim that today's print contains ZERO realized drag while the dependency ratio has deteriorated for a decade. I MOVE, worth ~+0.25 pp on the China leg. But I do NOT move the full distance: the red-teamer calls the drag \"back-loaded, with the steep part in the 2040s\", and that is only half right — the worker-to-retiree ratio going 4.4 -> 2.8 is a -36% collapse in the support ratio INSIDE this window, which is not back-loaded at all. What steepens in the 2040s is the old-age burden level, not the in-window ratio decline.\n(2) MEASURED VERSUS TRUE CHINESE GROWTH. CQ3 resolves on official PPP real GDP channelled through IMF WEO, and China's official real-GDP series is a smoothed administrative object. This is a genuine resolution-criterion hit and the most novel contribution in either Wave-2 seat. I MOVE, but by +0.3 pp rather than his implied +0.6-0.8 pp: China's own growth target is itself declining through the 2030s, and the 2018 census revision and the 2023-24 deflator episodes are both counterexamples to a pure upward-smoothing prior.\n\nOECD-38 LEG. Locked baseline 0.6%/yr per capita (post-aging) + ~0.2%/yr population = +0.80 pp. AI TFP lift probability-weighted off AI-Q6's OWN regime means: 0.162 x 0.75 + 0.472 x 0.35 + 0.365 x 0.07 = +0.31 pp, net +0.25 pp after aging-as-demand-pull (+0.05), fiscal crowd-out as net interest goes 2% -> 3% of GDP (-0.05) and incremental Baumol reallocation (-0.06). Capital services +0.13 pp — THE THIRD RED-TEAM HIT, partially accepted: impairment is a BOOK-VALUE event, a written-down GPU still computes and a written-down substation still delivers electrons, so measured capital services do not fall because a balance sheet is marked, and the Fermi halving (+0.14 -> +0.07) and the Scout's \"zero or negative\" are a category error. PARTIAL only, because AI-Q4's resolution includes CANCELLATIONS, and a cancelled buildout genuinely subtracts from investment flow; and its second limb (external revenue < 0.5x compute D&A) does bear on measured output in the AI-producing sector. Damages/energy-cost -0.08 pp. OECD-38 = 0.80 + 0.25 + 0.13 - 0.08 = 1.10%/yr.\n\nCHINA LEG, MEASURED. Build: labour input -1.15%/yr headcount x labour share 0.5 = -0.58; capital deepening +2.3 (41% I/Y against a sharply deteriorating ICOR and the property overhang); TFP +1.1 (>50% of world industrial robot installs, offset by local-government-debt and pension crowd-out); human capital +0.2; balance-sheet/deflation friction -0.3. True = ~3.0% after the double-count correction. Measurement uplift +0.3 -> MEASURED 3.25%.\n\nCOUPLED P50 = 0.66 x 1.10 + 0.34 x 3.25 = 0.726 + 1.105 = 1.83%, plus ~0.05 pp within-window weight drift = 1.88 -> P50 1.9%. THE ARITHMETIC THE BRIEF DEMANDS: China supplies 1.11 pp of the 1.9%, MORE THAN THE ENTIRE OECD-38 LEG's 0.73 pp. The coupled answer is mostly a China question, and after the two corrections it points UP from the Wave-2 Bayesian centre, not down.\n\nJONES/BAUMOL WEAKEST LINK, WHERE I TAKE THE FERMI SEAT'S FINDING OVER HIS OWN CONCLUSION. His CES arithmetic (automatable share 0.8, non-automatable 0.2, sigma = 0.5) gives an asymptotic LEVEL cap of ~5x that is hard but binds in the 2040s-50s, costing the OECD only ~0.15-0.30 pp/yr by 2036. A cap that does not bind inside the window cannot carry a 1.75-1.8% P50; the red-teamer is correct about that. What actually caps 2036 is DIFFUSION (AI-Q2 at 21.3% says fewer than half of OECD firms use AI in production) and PHYSICAL ENERGIZATION, and both are already inside my +0.25 pp TFP term.\n\nBAND. P10 = 0.9: an OECD-wide contraction inside the window plus a Chinese property/pension accident printing sub-2.5% measured; a 5-year average smooths, so 0.9 needs two genuinely bad years. P90 = 2.9: measured China ~4.5% with OECD-38 at ~2.1%. I hold P90 ABOVE the Bayesian's 2.65 and below the red-teamer's 3.2. The energization LR (transformer lead times >160 weeks, turbines five years, 36-84 month lead-time-to-power; capacity operational in 2032-36 must be queued by 2029-32) is the only above-2:1 downside signal in the effort and it DOES cap the energy-led right tail — but the Bayesian applied it to the whole right tail, and the measured-China channel bypasses it entirely, which is why 2.65 is too tight. Implied sigma: right 0.78, left 0.80, with a fatter outer left tail beyond P10 for the capex-bust and private-credit branches (Fitch US private-credit default rate 9.5% in July 2026 after 9.4% in June and 6.9% a year earlier, described as a record; median non-accruals at the 20 largest public BDCs 2.8% of cost in Q2 2026 vs 2.0% at end-March, SOLVE via FT — caveat preserved: Octus puts aggregate BDC non-accruals at $9.5B, 1.9% of reported debt investments, DOWN 5% from $10B in Q1 2026, and KBRA reads it as normalisation rather than deterioration; the vendor series conflict).\n\nSIMULATION: I take no point estimate. Its four disclosed structural biases all run one way (no implementation of the locked -2.7 pp China drag, gamma = 0.55 on useful energy so its own electrification build manufactures growth, no price level or failable demand side so the CQ6-D cell is unrepresentable and the distribution is truncated from below, and an OECD-38 proxy of us + oecd-ex-us that drops Japan, Korea, Türkiye, Mexico, Chile, Colombia and Costa Rica). Every reading is also UN-WPP-low-conditional, which over THIS window is growth-FLATTERING rather than damaging, since post-2025 births do not reach the labour force until the 2040s while low fertility lightens young dependency now.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:59.829Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Kills the load-bearing plank of my entire payload. CQ2 falls from 44 to ~26 (conjunct A from 0.76 to ~0.48). CQ6 inverts in its low-growth half: C from 27 to ~16, D from 12 to ~22, because the <2.00% condition that currently starves D becomes live. In the asset matrix, probability mass shifts from C to D, which flips G7 long nominal sovereigns from my worst conditional row (-6.5 in C) toward my best (+6.0 in D), and cuts gold's weighted contribution hard. This is the cheapest single observation that falsifies me, and it is a measurement question, not a macro one.\",\"signpost\":\"The OECD's own published GDP-weighted OECD-total core inflation aggregate (OECD Main Economic Indicators / CPI database) prints below 2.0% y/y for four consecutive quarters, with Türkiye's core in single digits and Mexico's below 3.5% — i.e. the five EM members do NOT hold the aggregate above the G7 core.\"} | {\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Refutes the MOVE level-scaling argument EMPIRICALLY rather than theoretically, which is the whole of my move above the Wave-1 centre. CQ5 falls from 26 to ~14 (conjunct A from 0.50 back to ~0.30). CQ4 P90 falls from 3.4 to ~3.0 on a smaller term-premium tail. CQ6-C falls ~3 points to B. Gold's C-column number falls ~1.5 pp and long nominal sovereigns improve ~1.0 pp in every column.\",\"signpost\":\"The ICE BofA MOVE Index three-year average over 2027-2029, taken from ICE's own index data rather than an aggregator, prints below 90 WHILE the G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal yield averages above 3.75% over the same three years.\"} | {\"by_when\":\"IMF WEO October 2029 edition\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Kills the measured-versus-true-China channel that is worth ~0.3 pp of my CQ3 P50 and ~5 points of my CQ6-A, and is the main place I moved toward the red-team. CQ3 P50 falls from 1.9 to ~1.7 and P90 from 2.9 to ~2.7; CQ6-A falls from 18 to ~12 with the mass going to B (+5) and D (+1). China equities/onshore duration: the equity half is damaged in every column and the duration half improves. It would also partly vindicate the Bayesian seat's narrower P90 of 2.65 against mine.\",\"signpost\":\"IMF WEO prints combined 2030 and 2031 China real GDP growth below 3.5%/yr on the REALIZED/ESTIMATE line (not the forecast line) — i.e. the official series stops printing near its target.\"} | {\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies BOTH places I departed upward from the Wave-1 CQ4 centre: the non-US-convergence delta term (+0.20 pp) and my cut of the Reinhart-Sbrancia repression wedge from -0.20 to -0.14. CQ4 P50 falls from 2.2 to ~1.4 and P90 from 3.4 to ~2.9; P10 falls from 1.15 to ~0.6, restoring the repression tail that the 3:1 TIPS-anchor likelihood ratio currently cuts. CQ5 falls from 26 to ~17 via the rate-level channel into the MOVE floor. CQ6-D rises from 12 to ~19. G7 long nominals, TIPS and cash are all mis-priced too low in my 2026-2030 buildout column.\",\"signpost\":\"The G7 GDP-weighted 10y sovereign real yield (10y nominal minus 10y inflation swap) averages below 1.00% for twelve consecutive months, with the 10y JGB real yield still below 0.50%.\"} | {\"by_when\":\"Jun 30, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Removes the physical-energization cap that holds my CQ3 P90 at 2.9 and that makes power/grid (+7.5) and construction/trades (+6.5) my two best 2026-2030 rows. CQ3 P90 rises to ~3.2 and P50 to ~2.0; CQ6-A rises from 18 to ~24 with B falling. My upward correction to AI-Q4 (toward 0.73) reverses toward the locked 0.66, and realized AI-Q3 load returns toward the locked 998 TWh P50. In the matrix, power/grid buildout falls from 7.5 to ~4.5 and construction from 6.5 to ~4.0 as the scarcity rent evaporates, while the AI-complex column rises ~3 pp in A and B.\",\"signpost\":\"Generator step-up transformer lead times fall back below 80 weeks AND gas turbine lead times below 36 months, on OEM order-book disclosures or NERC/EIA reporting — against >160 weeks and five years respectively as of Q1 2026.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 3 reconciler seat, effort 'growth'."}}